  "question": {
    "text": "<p>As shown in figures, three identical polaroids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are placed one after another. The pass axis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are inclined at angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> with respect to axis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. The source <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> has an intensity of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>256</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>.&nbsp;The intensity of light at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> is _____<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0055_8508fe5b67b9.png\" style=\"width: 700px; height: 196px;\" />&nbsp;</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "24",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>As given here, the pass axis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are inclined at angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> with respect to axis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>.&nbsp;</p><p>Therefore, intensity of light after falling on first polaroid&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Intensity of light after falling on second&nbsp;polaroid&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Intensity of light after falling on third&nbsp;polaroid&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>32</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>The intensity of light at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>32</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mn>32</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>"
  }
}